his name is anwar raslan today a german  court sentenced the former syrian  colonel to life in prison for crimes  against humanity prosecutors accused him  of running a detention center for the  assad regime and of overseeing the  torture of 4 000 people including 60  deaths ruslan became a refugee and  arrived here in germany in 2014. he  thought that he had left the civil war  and his dark secret behind him and he  did  for a while but today his past and the  forces of justice  they finally caught up with him  and for reaction to today's sentencing  i'm joined now by kenneth rothy the  executive director for human rights  watch kenneth it's good to have you back  on  the day you and i have spoke numerous  times about efforts to bring justice to  those who who have and who continue to  commit war crimes and crimes against  humanity in syria how important is  today's sentencing of anwar  rosslyn  this is a truly historic moment it's the  first  judgment of its sort and to put this in  perspective the assad government in  syria has pursued this war really  through a war crime strategy that's been  the entire way they fought the war they  have deliberately bombed hospitals and  schools and marketplaces they've used  chemical weapons against their own  people  they've used starvation as a method of  war and the issue here in this  this trial was the pervasive use of  arbitrary detention torture and indeed  thousands of executions now  the assad government denies all of this  the obvious place to have held a trial  would have been the international  criminal court but syria never joined  the court and the only route to the  court then would have been through the  u.n security council which russia and  china blocked with their veto  so  a number of governments germany included  helped to circumvent the veto and the  u.n security council by going to the u.n  general assembly and getting it to  create what's known as the international  independent impartial mechanism or the  triple i m for syria which is um  basically a prosecutor without  jurisdiction so there is today a french  prosecutor sitting in geneva collecting  evidence of these atrocities and and  making it possible for you know a  prosecutor in a a small town like  koblenz to pursue a case like this  because in fact there are now reams of  records about the atrocities about the  chain of command and so um it is  possible to exercise this concept of  universal jurisdiction that is to say  you know the ability to pursue things  like war crimes crimes against humanity  torture in any available court  that becomes more possible now because  of this repository of evidence sitting  in geneva that was an important part of  the trial of koblenz and this is now an  historic step forward an actual fair  trial and a conviction and kenneth what  you're saying is that we would maybe  have  you know gotten to this verdict earlier  we would be seeing even more trials  if it weren't for china and russia  standing in their way  are you saying that they are protecting  war criminals inside of syria  well it's worse than that i mean yes  china's protecting war criminals russia  is participating in the war crimes if  you look at the bombing today or until  quite recently of the hospitals and the  schools and the markets and the  apartment buildings um more often these  days that's russian jets rather than  syrian jets so you know russia is  completely a partner in the crimes of  the air war you know russia isn't as  present on the ground for the kind of  torture that was the focus in koblenz  but russia has a direct you know  personal interest in avoiding  accountability for these war crimes  because it is an active participant in  these crimes why aren't we calling them  out there more at the human security  council for example  well the u.n security council can't  speak  because of the russian veto  so it is it's been in fact very  difficult even for the security council  to authorize cross-borders humanitarian  assistance from turkey into the parts of  syria still held by the armed opposition  you know russia very reluctantly goes  along and has gradually been reducing  the entrances to syria down to one um so  but to go beyond that to to bring in the  international criminal court to condemn  the atrocities in syria russia  absolutely precludes that and that's a  real weakness of the structure of the  security council you know the five  permanent members the u.s um china  russia france and britain  each of them has a veto each can prevent  a resolution from going forward they're  not supposed to use this in this kind of  narrow self-interested way they're not  supposed to defend the perpetrators of  mass atrocities but that's exactly what  russia and china have been doing in the  case of syria and elsewhere i should say  places like me  you know more than 2500 miles away from  where we are right now in syria the  misery for millions who are still living  under assad that misery continues and  it's faded a lot from the public's radar  we have to admit that what does today's  landmark court case what does it mean  for those syrians  well you know one important element of  this is there are certain countries  denmark is a good example which want to  pretend that it's safe to send syrian  refugees back to syria because in most  of syria the war is over it's just you  know the assad government back to its  usual repressive ways but you know human  rights watch has done investigations  where we found that you know people who  um go back to syria a relative handful  of people are treated as suspicious just  because they left and they are subject  to the same detention centers the same  torture the same risk of atrocities so  um you know what we saw today in kuwait  the focus of this trial is an ongoing  reality and we should remember this i  mean germany's been very generous in  allowing in syrian refugees but other  countries just can't wait to send them  back and that is completely wrong  because it would be sending them back to  the persecution that was the heart of  this trial  kenneth roth with human rights watch ken  it's always good talking with you  valuable valuable context to help us  understand what's what continues to go  on inside and outside of syria thank you  thank you
 

